1
00:00:02,797 --> 00:00:07,960
For eleven years, I had not seen Joe nor Biddy with my bodily eyes,—though they had both been

2
00:00:07,960 --> 00:00:14,281
often before my fancy in the East,—when, upon an evening in December, an hour or two after dark,

3
00:00:14,281 --> 00:00:19,718
I laid my hand softly on the latch of the old kitchen door. I touched it so softly

4
00:00:19,718 --> 00:00:26,358
that I was not heard, and looked in unseen. There, smoking his pipe in the old place by the kitchen

5
00:00:26,358 --> 00:00:34,281
firelight, as hale and as strong as ever, though a little grey, sat Joe; and there, fenced into

6
00:00:34,281 --> 00:00:41,600
the corner with Joe’s leg, and sitting on my own little stool looking at the fire, was—I again!

7
00:00:41,601 --> 00:00:46,641
“We giv’ him the name of Pip for your sake, dear old chap,” said Joe, delighted,

8
00:00:46,641 --> 00:00:51,077
when I took another stool by the child’s side (but I did not rumple his hair),

9
00:00:51,077 --> 00:00:56,679
“and we hoped he might grow a little bit like you, and we think he do.”

10
00:00:56,679 --> 00:01:02,116
I thought so too, and I took him out for a walk next morning, and we talked immensely,

11
00:01:02,116 --> 00:01:07,835
understanding one another to perfection. And I took him down to the churchyard, and set him

12
00:01:07,835 --> 00:01:13,116
on a certain tombstone there, and he showed me from that elevation which stone was sacred

13
00:01:13,116 --> 00:01:23,116
to the memory of Philip Pirrip, late of this Parish, and Also Georgiana, Wife of the Above.

14
00:01:23,116 --> 00:01:27,320
“Biddy,” said I, when I talked with her after dinner, as her little girl lay sleeping in

15
00:01:27,320 --> 00:01:32,960
her lap, “you must give Pip to me one of these days; or lend him, at all events.”

16
00:01:32,960 --> 00:01:37,437
“No, no,” said Biddy, gently. “You must marry.”

17
00:01:37,437 --> 00:01:42,797
“So Herbert and Clara say, but I don’t think I shall, Biddy. I have so settled

18
00:01:42,797 --> 00:01:50,077
down in their home, that it’s not at all likely. I am already quite an old bachelor.”

19
00:01:50,077 --> 00:01:55,281
Biddy looked down at her child, and put its little hand to her lips, and then put the good

20
00:01:55,281 --> 00:02:00,156
matronly hand with which she had touched it into mine. There was something in the action,

21
00:02:00,156 --> 00:02:05,639
and in the light pressure of Biddy’s wedding-ring, that had a very pretty eloquence in it.

22
00:02:05,641 --> 00:02:10,516
“Dear Pip,” said Biddy, “you are sure you don’t fret for her?”

23
00:02:10,516 --> 00:02:13,398
“O no,—I think not, Biddy.”

24
00:02:13,398 --> 00:02:18,195
“Tell me as an old, old friend. Have you quite forgotten her?

25
00:02:18,195 --> 00:02:23,835
“My dear Biddy, I have forgotten nothing in my life that ever had a foremost place there,

26
00:02:23,835 --> 00:02:28,601
and little that ever had any place there. But that poor dream,

27
00:02:28,601 --> 00:02:34,516
as I once used to call it, has all gone by, Biddy,—all gone by!”

28
00:02:34,516 --> 00:02:38,960
Nevertheless, I knew, while I said those words, that I secretly intended to revisit

29
00:02:38,960 --> 00:02:49,000
the site of the old house that evening, alone, for her sake. Yes, even so. For Estella’s sake.

30
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:54,476
I had heard of her as leading a most unhappy life, and as being separated from her husband,

31
00:02:54,476 --> 00:03:00,116
who had used her with great cruelty, and who had become quite renowned as a compound of pride,

32
00:03:00,116 --> 00:03:06,195
avarice, brutality, and meanness. And I had heard of the death of her husband,

33
00:03:06,195 --> 00:03:11,281
from an accident consequent on his ill-treatment of a horse. This release

34
00:03:11,281 --> 00:03:16,835
had befallen her some two years before; for anything I knew, she was married again.

35
00:03:16,835 --> 00:03:22,835
The early dinner hour at Joe’s, left me abundance of time, without hurrying my talk with Biddy,

36
00:03:22,835 --> 00:03:29,156
to walk over to the old spot before dark. But, what with loitering on the way to look at old

37
00:03:29,156 --> 00:03:35,835
objects and to think of old times, the day had quite declined when I came to the place.

38
00:03:35,835 --> 00:03:40,320
There was no house now, no brewery, no building whatever left,

39
00:03:40,320 --> 00:03:46,195
but the wall of the old garden. The cleared space had been enclosed with a rough fence,

40
00:03:46,195 --> 00:03:51,554
and looking over it, I saw that some of the old ivy had struck root anew, and was growing green

41
00:03:51,554 --> 00:03:59,398
on low quiet mounds of ruin. A gate in the fence standing ajar, I pushed it open, and went in.

42
00:03:59,398 --> 00:04:06,156
A cold silvery mist had veiled the afternoon, and the moon was not yet up to scatter it. But,

43
00:04:06,156 --> 00:04:10,679
the stars were shining beyond the mist, and the moon was coming,

44
00:04:10,679 --> 00:04:17,156
and the evening was not dark. I could trace out where every part of the old house had been,

45
00:04:17,156 --> 00:04:24,475
and where the brewery had been, and where the gates, and where the casks. I had done so,

46
00:04:24,476 --> 00:04:30,960
and was looking along the desolate garden walk, when I beheld a solitary figure in it.

47
00:04:30,960 --> 00:04:37,116
The figure showed itself aware of me, as I advanced. It had been moving towards me,

48
00:04:37,116 --> 00:04:44,875
but it stood still. As I drew nearer, I saw it to be the figure of a woman. As I drew nearer

49
00:04:44,875 --> 00:04:51,000
yet, it was about to turn away, when it stopped, and let me come up with it. Then,

50
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:56,398
it faltered, as if much surprised, and uttered my name, and I cried out,—

51
00:04:56,398 --> 00:04:58,241
“Estella!”

52
00:04:58,241 --> 00:05:02,195
“I am greatly changed. I wonder you know me.”

53
00:05:02,195 --> 00:05:07,398
The freshness of her beauty was indeed gone, but its indescribable majesty and

54
00:05:07,398 --> 00:05:13,796
its indescribable charm remained. Those attractions in it, I had seen before;

55
00:05:13,797 --> 00:05:18,875
what I had never seen before, was the saddened, softened light of the once

56
00:05:18,875 --> 00:05:26,922
proud eyes; what I had never felt before was the friendly touch of the once insensible hand.

57
00:05:26,922 --> 00:05:32,875
We sat down on a bench that was near, and I said, “After so many years, it is strange that

58
00:05:32,875 --> 00:05:40,718
we should thus meet again, Estella, here where our first meeting was! Do you often come back?”

59
00:05:40,718 --> 00:05:42,758
“I have never been here since.”

60
00:05:42,758 --> 00:05:47,601
“Nor I.” The moon began to rise, and I

61
00:05:47,601 --> 00:05:54,241
thought of the placid look at the white ceiling, which had passed away. The moon began to rise,

62
00:05:54,241 --> 00:06:00,718
and I thought of the pressure on my hand when I had spoken the last words he had heard on earth.

63
00:06:00,718 --> 00:06:05,281
Estella was the next to break the silence that ensued between us.

64
00:06:05,281 --> 00:06:08,437
“I have very often hoped and intended to come back,

65
00:06:08,437 --> 00:06:14,758
but have been prevented by many circumstances. Poor, poor old place!”

66
00:06:14,758 --> 00:06:18,437
The silvery mist was touched with the first rays of the moonlight,

67
00:06:18,437 --> 00:06:23,241
and the same rays touched the tears that dropped from her eyes. Not knowing that

68
00:06:23,241 --> 00:06:28,195
I saw them, and setting herself to get the better of them, she said quietly,—

69
00:06:28,195 --> 00:06:33,600
“Were you wondering, as you walked along, how it came to be left in this condition?”

70
00:06:33,601 --> 00:06:35,516
“Yes, Estella.”

71
00:06:35,516 --> 00:06:41,641
“The ground belongs to me. It is the only possession I have not relinquished. Everything

72
00:06:41,641 --> 00:06:47,195
else has gone from me, little by little, but I have kept this. It was the subject

73
00:06:47,195 --> 00:06:51,835
of the only determined resistance I made in all the wretched years.”

74
00:06:51,835 --> 00:06:53,875
“Is it to be built on?”

75
00:06:53,875 --> 00:07:00,960
“At last, it is. I came here to take leave of it before its change. And you,” she said,

76
00:07:00,960 --> 00:07:06,358
in a voice of touching interest to a wanderer,—“you live abroad still?”

77
00:07:06,358 --> 00:07:07,679
“Still.”

78
00:07:07,679 --> 00:07:10,077
“And do well, I am sure?”

79
00:07:10,077 --> 00:07:14,516
“I work pretty hard for a sufficient living, and therefore—yes, I do well.”

80
00:07:15,875 --> 00:07:19,320
“I have often thought of you,” said Estella.

81
00:07:19,320 --> 00:07:20,797
“Have you?”

82
00:07:20,797 --> 00:07:26,758
“Of late, very often. There was a long hard time when I kept far from me the remembrance

83
00:07:26,758 --> 00:07:32,281
of what I had thrown away when I was quite ignorant of its worth. But since my duty has

84
00:07:32,281 --> 00:07:39,116
not been incompatible with the admission of that remembrance, I have given it a place in my heart.”

85
00:07:39,116 --> 00:07:42,398
“You have always held your place in my heart,” I answered.

86
00:07:42,398 --> 00:07:44,437
And we were silent again until she spoke.

87
00:07:45,758 --> 00:07:48,476
“I little thought,” said Estella, “that I should

88
00:07:48,476 --> 00:07:54,241
take leave of you in taking leave of this spot. I am very glad to do so.”

89
00:07:54,241 --> 00:08:00,320
“Glad to part again, Estella? To me, parting is a painful thing. To me,

90
00:08:00,320 --> 00:08:05,514
the remembrance of our last parting has been ever mournful and painful.”

91
00:08:05,516 --> 00:08:12,601
“But you said to me,” returned Estella, very earnestly, “‘God bless you, God forgive you!’

92
00:08:12,601 --> 00:08:18,398
And if you could say that to me then, you will not hesitate to say that to me now,—now,

93
00:08:18,398 --> 00:08:22,000
when suffering has been stronger than all other teaching, and has taught me

94
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:28,000
to understand what your heart used to be. I have been bent and broken, but—I hope—into a

95
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:34,516
better shape. Be as considerate and good to me as you were, and tell me we are friends.”

96
00:08:34,516 --> 00:08:39,758
“We are friends,” said I, rising and bending over her, as she rose from the bench.

97
00:08:39,758 --> 00:08:44,000
“And will continue friends apart,” said Estella.

98
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:50,116
I took her hand in mine, and we went out of the ruined place; and, as the morning mists

99
00:08:50,116 --> 00:08:56,039
had risen long ago when I first left the forge, so the evening mists were rising now, and in all

100
00:08:56,039 --> 00:09:05,601
the broad expanse of tranquil light they showed to me, I saw no shadow of another parting from her.

101
00:09:05,601 --> 00:09:07,116
Thank you for listening.

102
00:09:07,116 --> 00:09:11,116
If you like our recordings consider liking this video and subscribing to our channel,

103
00:09:11,116 --> 00:09:14,358
so you don't miss any more audiobooks.
